course: medicine
lesson: 70
title: The kid who is always on the bench
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sam in Leeds sits on the bench all match in his kit, getting colder, and asks «Did I even play?»; coaches pick for many reasons (positions, who trained, who's played most), so sitting out once is not a verdict on you.
explain: Cold muscles are stiffer and strain more easily, so when Fionn in Dublin («Jaysus, I'm frozen») is waved on after 50 minutes, he jogs and does moving stretches on the touchline first, even for 10 minutes of play.
explain: Skill grows from many small sessions: Lucas in Ontario does 20 minutes of keepy-uppies every day, and Anaya in London asks her coach what to work on and passes against the garden wall, until the brain wires the move in.
explain: Fair minutes make everyone better: Ella's coach in Bath has 10 players for 7 places and rotates them, and if you never get on, it's fine to find a club where you play most of every match.
explain: Kids grow at very different times, so small at 10 tells little about 16; meanwhile a good sub watches, cheers and stays ready, because many games are won by players coming off the bench.
fact: Coaches choose who starts for many reasons, such as positions, who came to training and who has played most.
fact: Cold muscles are stiffer and get strained more easily than warm ones.
fact: A few minutes of jogging and moving stretches warms the muscles up before going on.
fact: Practice repeats a movement until the brain wires it in, so it becomes easier and smoother.
fact: Asking the coach «What can I work on?» and practising that one thing usually helps.
fact: At grassroots level playing time is how children improve, and fair minutes keep everyone learning and coming back.
fact: Moving to a club where you actually play is a sensible choice, not giving up.
fact: The big growth spurt of puberty can come years earlier for one child than another, and many late bloomers catch up.
fact: Coaches notice attitude, and cheering and watching the game keep a sub ready to go on.
fact: Most children play for fun and friends, and the exercise counts whether you start or sit.
fact: Disappointment can tighten the throat muscles as you hold back tears; it fades, especially once you're moving.
fact: Subs often change games, and many matches are won by players coming off the bench.
fact: «Jaysus» is an Irish way of saying «Jesus!» in surprise; «ag» is a South African «oh»; «t'» in Yorkshire means «the».
exercise: Sam is on the bench all match. Does it mean the coach dislikes him? | No, it's usually about positions and planning | Yes, benches are for kids the coach hates | Yes, the worst player always sits | No, it means he is the best
exercise: What's a good question for Sam to ask the coach? | What can I work on to get more minutes? | Why do you hate me so much more than the others? | Can I be captain next week if I train hard? | Can my mum pick the team for the next match?
exercise: Fionn has sat 50 minutes in the cold. Why warm up before going on? | Cold muscles strain more easily | Warming up makes the clock slower | It shows off to the other team | Cold muscles are faster than warm ones
exercise: Fionn goes on at 50 minutes of a 60-minute game. How long will he play? | 10 minutes | 50 minutes | 60 minutes | 110 minutes
exercise: Lucas does 20 minutes of keepy-uppies a day. How does practice help? | The brain wires the movement in | It makes his legs grow longer and stronger | It only works if he does it on weekends | It makes the ball lighter and easier to kick
exercise: Some kids are «just born good at sport». What's truer? | Practice builds most of the skill you see | Skill comes only from your parents, through your genes | Practice can't change anything | Only tall kids can get better
exercise: Ella's coach rotates 10 players for 7 places. Why? | Fair minutes help everyone improve | To make the team lose on purpose for once | So the best kids get tired | Because the rules say so
exercise: 7 places × 50 minutes is 350 minutes. Shared fairly by 10 players, how long each? | 35 minutes | 50 minutes | 70 minutes | 7 minutes
exercise: Jayden plays 5 minutes a game all season and moves club. Is that giving up? | No, finding a club where you play is smart | Yes, you must never change club | Yes, he should sit for ten more years | No, but only adults may change club
exercise: Thabo is the smallest at 10 and always a sub. What's true? | His growth spurt may still be coming | He will always be the smallest in every team | Small kids never make good players | He should stop playing sport
exercise: Maisie is a sub and happy: «here for t' laughs». What is she right about? | Fun, friends and exercise are the point | Subs don't get any real exercise from a match | Only the starting players get to have fun | Nobody should ever play sport just for laughs
exercise: Kai sulks with his hood up on the bench. What does the coach see? | A player who isn't with the team | A player who badly wants to play | A player who is very tired | A player who is the captain
exercise: Anaya's coach says «your first touch». What does she do? | Practises that one skill every day | Changes to a different sport | Waits for the coach to change his mind | Practises everything but first touch
exercise: Ruairi gets a lump in his throat when he's a sub again. What is it? | Throat muscles tightening as he holds back tears | A sore throat from shouting | A sign he is getting a cold | A piece of his sandwich stuck there
exercise: 15 in the squad and 11 start. How many subs? | 4 | 26 | 11 | 5
exercise: Are subs really part of the team? | Yes, subs often change games | No, they just watch from the side | No, only the starting eleven count | Yes, but only in cup finals and derbies
test: «coach hates me, I'm always on the bench, it's rigged». What's the likelier reason? | Positions and planning, not dislike | The coach picks by star sign | The coach never even looks at the bench | Subs are chosen by the referee
test: Fionn: «it's only 10 minutes, no need to warm up». What would happen? | His cold muscles would be more likely to strain | He'd play better stiff and cold | Nothing, short games can't injure you | He'd get a free kick for being cold
test: Lucas: «gotta get better, eh» (Canadian «eh» = right?). He does 20 minutes a day for 5 days. How many minutes a week? | 100 | 25 | 20 | 200
test: Ella's mum: «proper fair, that». Another parent: «only the best should play at kids' level». Who's right? | Ella's mum; fair minutes make everyone better | The other parent; the best must always play | Neither; nobody should play | Both; it's the same thing
test: Thabo: «Ag, I'll grow, man.» Will the biggest kids at 10 be the best at 16? | Not necessarily; late bloomers often catch up | Yes, always, the order never changes after ten | Yes, because small kids stop growing | No, the smallest are always best
test: Kai on the bench: «whatever, this is mid» («mid» = rubbish, average). What should a good sub do? | Watch, cheer and stay ready | Put his headphones on and switch off | Go home early if he's not needed | Moan to the other team about his coach
test: In a sports film the sub sits all season, then comes on and wins the final. What's the real point? | Subs can change games, so stay ready | Subs only ever get on the pitch in films | Subs win every final if they're patient | Subs never get on the pitch in real life
test: «I'm never good enough, why even bother playing» says a kid who's usually a sub. What's true? | Fun, friends and fitness are the point | Only the best players should play sport at all | Subs don't count as players | Exercise only counts for starters
test: Anaya does 100 wall passes a day for 7 days. How many touches a week? | 700 | 107 | 100 | 70
test: Jayden's old club gave him 5 minutes a game; the new club gives him 40. What changes? | He gets far more practice in real games | Nothing, minutes on the pitch don't matter at all | He gets worse with more minutes | He has to stop training
test: Ruairi's lump in the throat fades once the warm-up starts. Why might that be? | Moving helps the stress reaction settle | Warm-ups cure all sore throats straight away | The coach changed the team sheet | He swallowed it with water
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a player who has been inactive in cold conditions should warm up before entering play. | Cold muscles are more susceptible to strain | Warm-up is required solely for spectators | Cold muscles are invariably faster | Warm-up reduces the duration of the match
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the development of sporting skill? | It is principally acquired through repeated practice | It is fixed at birth and cannot be altered | It is determined by height alone | It is conferred by selection to the starting team
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning children of small stature at the age of ten. | The pubertal growth spurt may occur considerably later | Such children will invariably remain small | Such children are unsuited to sport | Their adult height is fixed at that age
exam: It is given that a squad comprises fifteen players of whom eleven commence the match. Determine the number of substitutes. | Four | Eleven | Twenty-six | Five
